[repost from efc-talk]

  Wild West or Prudish Prairies?

Advocates of free speech may cringe at the chill that seems to be growing
in Winnipeg.  Canada's wild west is looking pretty prudish these days.

Recently, Mr. Pagtakhan, MP for Winnipeg-North, introduced a private
members motion in Parliament calling for some limits to be placed
on what can be communicated through computer networks. [1]

In a move that seems related to this sentiment, an internet service provider
called "Magic Online Services" (http://www.magic.mb.ca) has recently dropped
about 20 newsgroups from their Usenet service.  Most of the newsgroups were
from the "alt.sex.*" hierarchy.

The decision to censor was made following conversations between Magic staff
and the RCMP.  Apparently the RCMP advised that there were some legal
concerns about some of the articles in some of the "sex" newsgroups.
Whatever was said, it was enough to prompt the removal of several newsgroups.

Like many other internet service providers, this one isn't willing
to serve as a "test case" to decide how Canada's laws apply to cyberspace.

There's now a familiar refrain being repeated by internet service providers
who've acquiesced to pressures to censor.  "People can always get the stuff
from other sites anyway, so it doesn't matter we've cut these newsgroups".
True enough, perhaps, but how many of us would accept this excuse if
our local library pulled 20 books off the shelves?


- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
[1] Mr. Pagtakhan's motion was:

   "That, in the opinion of this House, the government should move
   with speed to adopt legislative measures aimed at stopping the
   spread of hate propaganda via the electronic Information Highway
   while simultaneously preserving legitimate use of the
   freedom of speech and expression."  (motion M-384)

I asked Mr. Pagtakhan's staff several questions about the intent
of this motion.  I was told I can expect a reply in about a week,
after they've formulated their answers.

