> To believe that the instruction set contained in the DNA / RNA came
> about by mere chance ?

Current theories of abiogenesis posit that it didn't come about by
"mere" chance, although randomness played a part.  For example, the
laws of chemistry and physics strongly influence what replicators
can be made of.

>Yeah, I seem to remember that the simplest bacteria has something on the
>order of 3000 base pairs within it's DNA. What would be the probability
>of those base pairs coming together by some random chance. I believe
>that it would be 
>
>   4! X 3000!

Your belief is wrong, and you have a cartoon understanding of evolution.

First, the base pairs didn't come together solely by "random chance";
once a replicator exists, natural selection acts on it.  Second,
like the famous example of a deal of cards, where any particular deal
is very unlikely, but the probability of some deal is 1, there is no reason to
believe than any particular bacterium was pre-ordained.  Start with a
different replicator, and a different environment, and a different
single-celled creature would have arisen.

Bogus probability calculations like yours are debunked at length in
_Scientists Confront Creationism_, chapter 5.

This newsgroup [alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk] is probably not the best
place for discussion of evolution and abiogenesis; I've set the followups
to talk.origins.

>Jim Decker

