Date: 6 Mar 95 12:58:32 -0500

Our University Senate is review some parts of the Bill of Rights and
Responsibilities in our Faculty and Profession Personnel Handbook in
response to work by the Academic Computing Resources Subcomittee to
extend that statement to cover behavior in cyberspace, a term I am
using broadly to cover any use of computers and networks.

The Subcommittee has taken the position that behavior in cyberspace is
behavior and should not be treated under a separate Bill of Rights and
Responsibilities, a position I share.  The question that arises is how
to handle those matters that are specific to cyberspace, like
spamming, and the degree to which those forms of behavior are, in
fact, unique.

I'd be interested in how other universities have addressed the
question of whether a single Bill of Rights and Responsibilities
should cover real and virtual environments.

