A recent situation has come up here on campus at the University of
Memphis, and discussion has been pretty much confined to the local
umem.* groups.  However, it could set a potentially dangerous
precedent for other student users at universities across the nation.
I'll try to keep this as brief as possible and stick to what we know.
The facts are not all clear yet, but hopefully the two students in
question will request that the hearings with Judicial Affairs be open
to the public.  Most of the information presented herein, including the
names of the students, has been available on the local newsgroups, as
well as in the campus newspaper. 

The first situation involves Ralph Handian, who has been a regular poster
to umem.general, umem.talk, and the notoriously tasteless umem.personals for
several straight semesters.  He has posted messages of varying vulgarity,
some exceedingly descriptive, many times before.  Some of his most recent
were entitled "Will Trade Math Tutoring for Sex".  In his interview with
the Daily Helmsman he stated that he found these at various points on the
'Net, removed the headers, and reposted them.  He received a letter signed
by Assistant Dean Kathryn Story (kstory@cc.memphis.edu) in Judicial Affairs,
that said he had violated the Student Conduct Code, particularly the section
regarding sending or receiving obscene messages on campus computers.  His 
account has been frozen, pending review.

The second involves Dave Hooper, who also posts to the umem.groups.  Dave
sent several emails to WREG-TV Channel 3 Memphis (wregtv3@aol.com), 
criticizing their recent news series on the Internet, and using various
profanities.  Someone faked the name and email address of one of the news
personalities and posted fairly vulgar responses to Dave's emails.
Apparently, someone at WREG-TV contacted the Media Relations Department on
campus, and Dave's account was frozen as well.  He also received a letter
informing him that he was under review for violation of the Student Conduct
Code.

My personal questions regarding the incident are as follows.  I have been
posting them to the local groups, and forwarding them to Dean Story and
WREG-TV3.

1. Why weren't the two students given warnings before such disciplinary action
was taken?  We do not have reminders about the Student Conduct Code, in fact
I don't remember anything about it when I received my vax account.  The
minimum courtesy the administration could extend is to talk to the students
before shutting them down.

2. Obscenity laws are subject to local standards.  How the fuck can they
apply local standards to a global communications network?  With USENET, 
the standards of the "community" translate to the standards of each
newsgroup, and each group has its own standards, as well as its own ways of
enforcement.  Umem.personals has extremely low standards, and no other users
had ever protested Handian's posts, other than to flame him for being a 
deviant in general.

3. Why should WREG-TV have that kind of weight with the campus administration?
Why the hell would they get that upset over some vulgar email?  ARe they THAT
insecure?  Would they have had such a sphincter fit if Hooper had sent them
a snailmail through the U.S. postal system instead of an email?

4. Does the administration even realize how much 'obscene' material is 
available to the poor, weak, impressionable college students on the
global newsgroups?  I doubt it, they probably wouldn't know a uudecoder if
it jumped up and sodomized them.

5. Do they realize that citizens of all nations correspond and post to these
newsgroups and interact on IRC?  Do they think that these citizens give a
fuck about the anal-retentive regulations that the Bible-bashers of the U.S.
would like to apply to digital communications?

This is nothing like the U.Mich. incident.  No fellow classmates were named
in a snuff story.  This is just plain old vulgarity, and someone here in the
good ole Mid-South got their panties in a bunch.  Probably the same people
who will go down to the Ole Revival Grounds this weekend for a good ole
fashioned book-burning.

