I didn't see the original posting on creationism, but it may well be as
much drivel as this is. Neither Darwins theories nor current theories
regarding how species have evolved are "Materialistic Religion" or any
other form of religion. This is similar to the garbage that Louisianna
tried to get away with when they tried to say that schools should teach
Creationism as if it were just another scientific theory. What crap.
Today's theories regarding evolution will continue to change and evolve
themselves in response to new data and new information. Creationists don't
change their tune. That's why theirs is not science, and why it is not
taught as science in the classrooms. 

>      Looking at all the other planets and their forbidding and purely
>   material composition and then looking at this gem we call Earth,
>   it is ridiculous to believe that all we see, including Life, just
>   happened by chance.

Ridiculous to you, but not to many other people. 

  Yet, that is the Theory of Creation taught
>   in our public schools - to the exclusion of all others.
No, most public schools do not teach any theory of creation of the
universe. We often (and I'm one of "those" teachers) teach evolution...and
discuss various theories, but I don't know anybody who teaches a theory
that says the the universe was created by chance. There is no scientific
evidence to support who or how the universe was conceived.  There are
theories as to what happened once the universe began...but nobody can get
any data that can be tested to theorize as to WHY or HOW it actually was
begun. One can discuss big bang etc etc etc but why the whole thing
started is beyond any meaningful scientific study at this point...as far
as my limited understanding goes. See....Christians can say, "God made
it"...but scientists have no way to test whether or not that is true. We
can study what happened SINCE the universe was created, but can't study
what happened BEFORE it was created in any meaningful way. 


>      It is insolent to assert that within the bounds of our limited 
>   intelligence lie all the explanations of what has here occured.
Agreed

>      To me, it is obvious that what we have here is not just stupid
>   cause and effect but a Will Expression of the highest Order of
>   Life - God.
It may be obvious to you, but it is far from obvious to many people, and
because YOUR view represents a particular religious perspective, and
because the courts have said that schools will not represent particular
religious perspectives on such matters, we don't teach such beliefs. I for
one do not wish to teach every possible religious belief (and they are NOT
theories...just beliefs) on how the world was created. The religious
beliefs of many native peoples here in Alaska are  quite at odds with your
beliefs. If we were to teach the beliefs of the inhabitants of Alaska we
would be teaching quite a variety of creation stories. The Christian story
would just be one of them. Instead, we teach today's current thinking on
evolution, as best we can given the rapid changes that occur. 

> And no one is going to push that concept aside - in our
  schools or other social institutions.

Wrong! I am quite literally pushing your concept aside in our schools! I
am doing that because our local school board, as required by law, would
not allow the teaching of specific specific religious beliefs on the
evolution of our planet to the exclusion of others. I teach science, not
religion as do most schools.

